  "narrative": "**Pathway Disruption Narrative**\n\nThe nine-differential metabolome is anchored by a striking enrichment of **Pyrimidine metabolism (KEGG map00240)** — FDR 0.0, fold-enrichment 692.8 — with six of the nine metabolites (deoxyuridine, CDP, UMP, dihydrouracil, ureidopropionic acid, and deoxyribose 1-phosphate) matching this pathway. The same six compounds also drive the three top-ranking SMPDB disease signatures: Beta Ureidopropionase Deficiency, Dihydropyrimidinase Deficiency, and MNGIE (Mitochondrial Neurogastrointestinal Encephalopathy), all of which converge on the same pyrimidine-catabolic core.\n\n**Key driver metabolites and downstream relationships.** Uridine 5′-monophosphate (UMP, C00105) and CDP (C00112) sit in the biosynthetic arm of pyrimidine metabolism, while deoxyuridine (C00526), dihydrouracil (C00429), and ureidopropionic acid (C02642) represent the catabolic arm. KEGG graph traversal confirms that dihydrouracil is directly converted to ureidopropionic acid in a single enzymatic step — consistent with dihydropyrimidinase acting immediately upstream of ureidopropionic acid in the uracil degradation pathway. UMP is reachable from CDP via a four-hop bidirectional route, reflecting their position within the same reversible biosynthetic sequence.\n\n**Biological significance.** Disruption of pyrimidine catabolism is clinically associated with dihydropyrimidinase and ureidopropionase deficiencies, which impair clearance of uracil and dihydrouracil, leading to toxic accumulation. Elevated deoxyuridine additionally suggests thymidylate synthase flux may be compromised, which would impair DNA synthesis. The presence of deoxyribose 1-phosphate indicates active salvage pathway engagement, potentially as a compensatory response to nucleoside pool depletion.\n\nIn summary, the treatment group shows coordinated perturbations spanning both pyrimidine biosynthesis (UMP, CDP) and degradation (deoxyuridine, dihydrouracil, ureidopropionic acid), consistent with a metabolic state that disrupts DNA precursor balance and may reflect drug-induced interference with pyrimidine metabolism or a systemic inflammatory reconfiguration of nucleotide homeostasis.",
